it's not polite to talk about brown and  black people dying because rich white  people in America feel better about  themselves when the browner black people  don't get to use DDT I'm Katherine  Maggie award for recent TV and I'm here  today with Andrew Morris co-editor of a  new volume Silent Spring at 50 Andrew  thanks for being with us thanks for  having me tell me a little bit about  Rachel Carson who wrote the  environmentalist classic Silent Spring  50 years ago and why you felt like it  was time to take her on that well we  start with why we thought we should take  her on now it's the 50th anniversary of  the book that many people think founded  modern American environmentalism it  shifted the emphasis from conservation  to a more radical view of man outside of  nature and that's a view that continues  to dominate the American environmental  movement  we knew there would be a lot of a lot of  Tory things said about this and so we  wanted to give a more critical  assessment of it what do you think is  one area where Rachel Carson who  obviously is responsible for the  limiting of the use of DDT radically in  both in the United States and eventually  internationally what are some of the the  costs that her work has imposed that  people tend to overlook well the most  important cost comes from the limit on  the use of DDT and malaria control  Carson spent 250 pages talking about the  dangers of chemical used specifically  DDT used and created this marvelous  image of a Silent Spring where no birds  sang and scared the hell out of people  the result of that is millions and  millions of poor people in Africa and  Asia and Latin America died of malaria  and that's an enormous cost that  environmentalists don't ever want to  talk about in her discussion of DDT did  she have a point about the the ways that  it was being used or misused or did she  just screw the pooch on this one  entirely she was absolutely right about  one thing and that's that there were  massive subsidized spraying programs  going on in which the US Department of  Agriculture was blanketing the country  with not just DDT but other insecticides  in some very ill-considered  rain programs to control agricultural  pests and those programs paid no  attention to  property rights and out of that came  some well justified lawsuits against the  USDA which failed in which the courts  ruled that private property rights have  to get out of the way when the public  interest is at stake and surely if the  USDA is doing it then the public  interest is at stake and so she was  right about that we shouldn't have been  subsidizing a massive spring of people's  property against their will but that's  about the only thing she was right about  what charges can we ultimately lay at  Rachel Carson's door I mean I know that  doing a sort of strict death toll might  be an exaggeration but can you just kind  of give a quick point by point of you  know what are the changes that we can  trace pretty directly to Silent Spring  and and to what are the hopes for  reversing those or otherwise limiting  the damage well I I think the three  things that we should worry about and we  have chapters that address all these ARS  one is really is the death toll people  don't want to talk about it it's not  polite to talk about brown and black  people dying because rich white people  in America feel better about themselves  when the brown of black people don't get  to use DDT but that's that's the fact of  it we have denied other countries the  ability to use the single most effective  tool to stop malaria and so those  millions and millions of deaths I think  can be clearly laid at the door of  Silent Spring the second is that Carson  really was an advocate for  nationalization of environmental policy  and I think we can trace the  environmental movement that grew out of  Silent Spring really had an impact on  moving environmental policy out of the  state level to the federal level and  that's really been a cost we lost a lot  of flexibility and a lot of ability to  adapt to local conditions of that and  the third thing is that she was a big  pusher for this balance of nature idea  and that's not how conservation  biologists think about things today we  have a excellent chapter in the book by  a conservation biologist who points out  that that the whole balance of nature  the circle of life this is not the way  that biologists think about things but  that idea is firmly embedded in the  public psyche in part because of Rachel  Carson who are the who are the heirs of  Rachel Carson well the heirs of Rachel  Carson are the national environmental  organizations that want to control our  lives  Carson advocated a variety of  precautionary measures nothing should be  used without extensive testing she's the  she's in some sense the godmother of the  precautionary principle I don't think  there is regional carcinoma today and  that none of the modern environmental  writers write nearly as well as Rachel  Carson and none of them had the skill  and ability that she had or the moral  authority that she had they wish they  were Rachel Carson the the interesting  question would be what would have  happened if Rachel Carson had not died  of cancer she's not quite as radical as  some of her followers are and it's her  timely death in that sense saved them  from her ever having to reconsider some  of the ideas that she put forward in the  book and since she was a scientist I  think I'd like to think that maybe what  better information came along she would  have paid attention to it thank you very  much for speaking with Reason TV this is  andrew morris co-editor of violence  bring at fifty four reason TV i'm  katherine bengi ward  you
 

